displayPoset -- generates a PDF representation of a poset and attempts to display it
************************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        displayPoset P
        displayPoset(P, SuppressLabels => Boolean)
        displayPoset(P, PDFDirectory => String)
        displayPoset(P, Jitter => Boolean)
  * Inputs:
      * P, an instance of the type *note Poset: Poset,, 
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * SuppressLabels => a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,,
        default value true, whether to display or suppress the labels of the
        poset
      * Jitter => a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, default value
        false, whether to randomly jitter the poset vertices
      * PDFDirectory => a *note string: (Macaulay2Doc)String,, default value
        "", which gives the path for the PDF

Description
===========

This method generates a PDF of the Hasse Diagram of the Poset view LaTeX code
which uses TikZ.  The method attempts to display the PDF using *note show:
(Macaulay2Doc)show,. See *note texPoset: texPoset, for more about the
representation.

Normally, the vertices of the Poset are placed at regular intervals along
horizontal lines.  However, this can sometimes cause edges to appear in the
Hasse diagram that are not truly there.  The Jitter option can be used to
randomly shift the positions of the vertices horizontally, which can often
cause the edges to be more clear.

See also
========

  * *note outputTexPoset: outputTexPoset, -- writes a LaTeX file with a
    TikZ-representation of a poset
  * *note texPoset: texPoset, -- generates a string containing a TikZ-figure of
    a poset
  * *note Jitter: texPoset, -- generates a string containing a TikZ-figure of a
    poset
  * *note SuppressLabels: texPoset, -- generates a string containing a
    TikZ-figure of a poset
  * *note hasseDiagram: hasseDiagram, -- produces the Hasse diagram of a poset

