greeneKleitmanPartition -- computes the Greene-Kleitman partition of a poset
****************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        l = greeneKleitmanPartition P
        l = greeneKleitmanPartition(P, Strategy => "auto")
        l = greeneKleitmanPartition(P, Strategy => "chains")
        l = greeneKleitmanPartition(P, Strategy => "antichains")
        l = greeneKleitmanPartition(P, Strategy => "Britz-Fomin")
  * Inputs:
      * P, an instance of the type *note Poset: Poset,, 
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * Strategy => a *note string: (Macaulay2Doc)String,, default value
        "auto", one of "auto", "chains", "antichains", or "Britz-Fomin"
  * Outputs:
      * l, an instance of the type *note Partition: (Macaulay2Doc)Partition,,
        the Greene-Kleitman partition of $P$

Description
===========

The Greene-Kleitman partition $l$ of $P$ is the partition such that the sum of
the first $k$ parts of $l$ is the maximum number of elements in a union of $k$
*note chains: chains, in $P$.

+-----------------------------------------------+
|i1 : P = poset {{1,2},{2,3},{3,4},{2,5},{6,3}};|
+-----------------------------------------------+
|i2 : greeneKleitmanPartition P                 |
|                                               |
|o2 = Partition{4, 2}                           |
|                                               |
|o2 : Partition                                 |
+-----------------------------------------------+

The conjugate of $l$ has the same property, but with chains replaced by *note
antichains: antichains,.  Because of this, it is often better to count via
antichains instead of chains.  This can be done by passing "antichains" as the
Strategy.

+--------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : D = dominanceLattice 6;                                  |
+--------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : time greeneKleitmanPartition(D, Strategy => "antichains")|
| -- used 0.400095s (cpu); 0.290773s (thread); 0s (gc)         |
|                                                              |
|o4 = Partition{9, 2}                                          |
|                                                              |
|o4 : Partition                                                |
+--------------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : time greeneKleitmanPartition(D, Strategy => "chains")    |
| -- used 1.0811e-05s (cpu); 1.0399e-05s (thread); 0s (gc)     |
|                                                              |
|o5 = Partition{9, 2}                                          |
|                                                              |
|o5 : Partition                                                |
+--------------------------------------------------------------+

The Greene-Kleitman partition of the $n$ *note chain: chain, is the partition
of $n$ with $1$ part.

+-------------------------------------+
|i6 : greeneKleitmanPartition chain 10|
|                                     |
|o6 = Partition{10}                   |
|                                     |
|o6 : Partition                       |
+-------------------------------------+

When the Strategy is "auto", for small posets, the strategies "chains" and
"antichains" are used as a brute force search is performed. For larger posets,
a max-flow computation is performed according to [BF01].

References
==========

  * [BF01] Thomas Britz and Sergey Fomin, Finite Posets and Ferrer Shapes (see
    https://doi.org/10.1006/aima.2000.1966 ), Adv. Math., 158.1 (2001), pp.
    86-127.

See also
========

  * *note chains: chains, -- computes all chains of a poset
  * *note antichains: antichains, -- computes all antichains of a poset

Ways to use greeneKleitmanPartition:
====================================

  * "greeneKleitmanPartition(Poset)"

For the programmer
