texPoset -- generates a string containing a TikZ-figure of a poset
******************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        texPoset P
        texPoset(P, SuppressLabels => Boolean)
        texPoset(P, Jitter => Boolean)
        tex P
  * Inputs:
      * P, an instance of the type *note Poset: Poset,, 
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * SuppressLabels => a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,,
        default value true, whether to display or suppress the labels of the
        poset
      * Jitter => a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, default value
        false, whether to randomly jitter the poset vertices
  * Outputs:
      * S, a *note string: (Macaulay2Doc)String,, a TikZ-figure of $P$

Description
===========

This method creates a TikZ-figure of the Hasse diagram of the given poset which
can be included in a LaTeX file.  The representation places the vertices on
horizontal lines corresponding to the *note filtration: filtration, of the
poset.  The only displayed edges are the *note coveringRelations:
coveringRelations, which are oriented so that lower vertices less than higher
vertices.

The method attempts to display labels in a sane way, if they are not suppressed
by the SuppressLabels option. Note that the SuppressLabels option's default
value can be set in the "~/.Macaulay2/init-Posets.m2" file.

Further, sometimes the vertices of the poset line up in unfortunate ways that
causes edges to touch other vertices.  Using the Jitter option can relieve this
by adding a small random horizontal shift to each vertex.

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : texPoset booleanLattice 2                                               |
|                                                                             |
|o1 = \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1, vertices/.style={draw, fill=black, circle, |
|             \node [vertices] (0) at (-0+0,0){};                             |
|             \node [vertices] (1) at (-.75+0,1.33333){};                     |
|             \node [vertices] (2) at (-.75+1.5,1.33333){};                   |
|             \node [vertices] (3) at (-0+0,2.66667){};                       |
|     \foreach \to/\from in {0/1, 2/3, 0/2, 1/3}                              |
|     \draw [-] (\to)--(\from);                                               |
|     \end{tikzpicture}                                                       |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|inner sep=0pt}]                                                              |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : texPoset(booleanLattice 2, Jitter => true)                              |
|                                                                             |
|o2 = \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1, vertices/.style={draw, fill=black, circle, |
|             \node [vertices] (0) at (-0+.267814,0){};                       |
|             \node [vertices] (1) at (-.75+.202018,1.33333){};               |
|             \node [vertices] (2) at (-.75+1.58819,1.33333){};               |
|             \node [vertices] (3) at (-0+.189883,2.66667){};                 |
|     \foreach \to/\from in {0/1, 2/3, 0/2, 1/3}                              |
|     \draw [-] (\to)--(\from);                                               |
|     \end{tikzpicture}                                                       |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|inner sep=0pt}]                                                              |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

Caveat
======

Calling texPoset via the command *note tex: (Macaulay2Doc)tex, expects that no
options are given.

